● Tool Use with Claude API

  Tool use (function calling) lets Claude call external functions you define to fetch data or perform actions.

  ---
  How It Works

  User Message → Claude decides to use a tool → You execute it → Send result back → Claude responds

  ---
  Step 1 — Define Tools

  const tools = [
    {
      name: "get_weather",
      description: "Get current weather for a location",
      input_schema: {
        type: "object",
        properties: {
          location: { type: "string", description: "City name, e.g. Chennai, IN" }
        },
        required: ["location"]
      }
    }
  ];

  ---
  Step 2 — Send to Claude

  import Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";

  const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY });

  const response = await client.messages.create({
    model: "claude-opus-4-6",
    max_tokens: 1024,
    tools: tools,
    messages: [{ role: "user", content: "What's the weather in Chennai?" }]
  });

  ---
  Step 3 — Agentic Loop (handle tool calls)

  const messages = [{ role: "user", content: "What's the weather in Chennai?" }];

  let response = await client.messages.create({
    model: "claude-opus-4-6",
    max_tokens: 1024,
    tools: tools,
    messages: messages
  });

  // Loop until Claude gives a final text response
  while (response.stop_reason === "tool_use") {
    const toolUseBlocks = response.content.filter(b => b.type === "tool_use");

    // Add Claude's response to history
    messages.push({ role: "assistant", content: response.content });

    const toolResults = [];

    for (const tool of toolUseBlocks) {
      console.log(`Tool called: ${tool.name}`, tool.input);

      // Execute your actual function here
      const result = execute_tool(tool.name, tool.input);

      toolResults.push({
        type: "tool_result",
        tool_use_id: tool.id,   // must match Claude's tool call ID
        content: result
      });
    }

    // Send results back to Claude
    messages.push({ role: "user", content: toolResults });

    response = await client.messages.create({
      model: "claude-opus-4-6",
      max_tokens: 1024,
      tools: tools,
      messages: messages
    });
  }

  // Final answer
  const text = response.content.find(b => b.type === "text");
  console.log(text.text);

  ---
  Step 4 — Your Tool Executor

  function execute_tool(name: string, input: any): string {
    if (name === "get_weather") {
      return `Chennai: 34°C, sunny`; // call real API here
    }
    return "Tool not found";
  }

  ---
  Flow Diagram

  User asks question
         ↓
  Claude responds with stop_reason: "tool_use"
         ↓
  You extract tool name + input
         ↓
  You run the function (API, DB, etc.)
         ↓
  You send tool_result back to Claude
         ↓
  Claude gives final text response

  ---
  Key Rules

  ┌────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            Rule            │                             Detail                             │
  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ stop_reason === "tool_use" │ Claude wants to call a tool                                    │
  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ tool_use_id                │ Must match Claude's ID when returning results                  │
  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Multiple tools             │ Claude can call several at once — return all results together  │
  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Descriptions matter        │ Write clear descriptions so Claude knows when to use each tool │
  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
