> explain tool use with claude api in this case

● How Tool Use Works in Your weather.ts

  Here's exactly what happens when you run the file:

  ---
  The Flow

  You define tools → Send to Gemini → Gemini calls tools → You execute → Send results back → Gemini answers

  ---
  Step-by-Step Breakdown

  1. You define what tools are available
  const tools = [{
    functionDeclarations: [
      { name: "get_weather", ... },  // Tool 1
      { name: "get_time",    ... },  // Tool 2
    ]
  }];
  You're telling Gemini: "These are the functions you can call."

  ---
  2. User sends a message
  chat("What's the weather in Dubai? Also what time is it in Dubai?")

  ---
  3. Gemini decides to call tools

  Gemini reads the question, sees it needs weather + time data, and responds with tool call requests instead of a text
  answer:
  Tool called: get_weather { location: 'Dubai' }
  Tool called: get_time    { timezone: 'Asia/Dubai' }
  Gemini does NOT answer yet — it waits for your data.

  ---
  4. You execute the tools (the agentic loop)
  while (true) {
    const functionCalls = parts.filter(p => p.functionCall); // detect tool calls

    if (functionCalls.length === 0) break;  // no more tools → exit loop

    const functionResponses = functionCalls.map(part => {
      const result = execute_tool(part.functionCall.name, part.functionCall.args);
      //             ↑ YOUR function runs here — DB, API, mock data, anything

      return { functionResponse: { name, response: { result } } };
    });

    response = await chatSession.sendMessage(functionResponses); // send results back
  }

  ---
  5. Your mock function returns data
  function execute_tool(name, args) {
    if (name === "get_weather") return mock[args.location]; // "38°C, hot and sunny"
    if (name === "get_time")    return mock[args.timezone]; // "6:45 PM GST"
  }

  ---
  6. Gemini combines results and answers
  Gemini: The weather in Dubai is 38°C, hot and sunny.
          The time in Dubai is 6:45 PM GST.

  ---
  Why the while(true) Loop?

  Because Gemini can call tools multiple times in sequence:

  Round 1 → Gemini calls get_weather
  Round 2 → Gemini calls get_time
  Round 3 → Gemini has all data → gives final text → loop breaks

  ---
  Visual Summary

  ┌─────────────┐        tools defined       ┌─────────────┐
  │   Your App  │ ─────────────────────────► │   Gemini    │
  │             │                            │             │
  │             │ ◄──── tool call request ── │             │
  │             │                            │             │
  │  execute_   │                            │             │
  │  tool()     │ ─────── tool result ─────► │             │
  │  runs here  │                            │             │
  │             │ ◄──── final text answer ── │             │
  └─────────────┘                            └─────────────┘

  ---
  Key Concept

  Gemini never runs your code. It only decides which tool to call and what arguments to pass. You are always in control
  of actually executing the function. This makes it safe — Gemini just orchestrates, you execute.

