● Sampling in MCP (Model Context Protocol)

  ---
  What is Sampling?

  Sampling reverses the normal client-server relationship. Instead of clients always calling servers, sampling lets MCP
  servers request LLM completions from the client.

  Normal flow:     Client  ──request──►  Server
  Sampling flow:   Server  ──request──►  Client  ──►  LLM

  The server can use AI capabilities without needing its own API key.

  ---
  How It Works (Step by Step)

  1. Server sends sampling/createMessage request to client
           ↓
  2. Client shows prompt to USER for approval
           ↓
  3. User approves → Client sends to LLM (Claude, etc.)
           ↓
  4. LLM responds → Client shows response to USER
           ↓
  5. User approves → Client returns result to Server

  This keeps humans in control at every step.

  ---
  Request Format

  {
    "method": "sampling/createMessage",
    "params": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "content": {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Analyze this sales data and give insights."
          }
        }
      ],
      "modelPreferences": {
        "hints": [{ "name": "claude-sonnet-4-6" }],
        "costPriority":         0.3,
        "speedPriority":        0.5,
        "intelligencePriority": 0.8
      },
      "systemPrompt": "You are a data analyst.",
      "maxTokens": 500
    }
  }

  Response Format

  {
    "result": {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The sales peaked in Q3 with a 24% increase..."
      },
      "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
      "stopReason": "endTurn"
    }
  }

  ---
  TypeScript Implementation

  import { Server } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js";
  import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";

  const server = new Server({
    name: "sampling-demo",
    version: "1.0.0",
  });

  // Tool that uses sampling to call LLM
  server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, async (request) => {
    if (request.params.name === "analyze_data") {
      const data = request.params.arguments.data;

      // Server requests LLM completion FROM the client
      const response = await server.request({
        method: "sampling/createMessage",
        params: {
          messages: [{
            role: "user",
            content: {
              type: "text",
              text: `Analyze this data:\n${data}`
            }
          }],
          modelPreferences: {
            hints: [{ "name": "claude-sonnet-4-6" }],
            intelligencePriority: 0.8,  // prefer smart model
            speedPriority: 0.5,
            costPriority: 0.3
          },
          systemPrompt: "You are a data analyst.",
          maxTokens: 500
        }
      });

      return {
        content: [{
          type: "text",
          text: response.result.content.text
        }]
      };
    }
  });

  const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
  await server.connect(transport);

  ---
  Model Preferences Explained

  ┌──────────────────────┬───────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
  │       Priority       │   Value   │            Meaning             │
  ├──────────────────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
  │ costPriority         │ 0.0 - 1.0 │ Higher = prefer cheaper models │
  ├──────────────────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
  │ speedPriority        │ 0.0 - 1.0 │ Higher = prefer faster models  │
  ├──────────────────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
  │ intelligencePriority │ 0.0 - 1.0 │ Higher = prefer smarter models │
  └──────────────────────┴───────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Content Types Supported

  // Text
  { type: "text", text: "Hello" }

  // Image
  { type: "image", data: "base64...", mimeType: "image/jpeg" }

  // Audio
  { type: "audio", data: "base64...", mimeType: "audio/wav" }

  ---
  Why Use Sampling?

  ┌───────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │      Benefit      │                     Detail                     │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No API key needed │ Server borrows client's LLM access             │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Human oversight   │ User approves every prompt & response          │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Agentic behavior  │ Server can make AI decisions mid-execution     │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Flexible models   │ Client picks best model based on priorities    │
  ├───────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Nested AI calls   │ LLM can call tools that trigger more LLM calls │
  └───────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Normal Tool Use vs Sampling

  Normal Tool Use:
    Claude ──calls──► your tool ──returns data──► Claude answers

  Sampling:
    Your MCP server ──asks Claude──► Claude thinks ──► server uses answer

  Sampling makes your MCP server the one asking Claude questions, not the other way around.

