● Roots in MCP (Model Context Protocol)

  ---
  What is a Root?

  A root defines the boundaries of where an MCP server is allowed to operate — like telling the server "you can only
  access files inside this folder".

  Root = A URI boundary the server is allowed to work within

  ---
  Why Roots Exist

  Without roots, an MCP server could access anything on your system. Roots restrict access to only what's needed — for
  security and focus.

  Without roots:  Server can read C:\Users\SriniAchuthan\** (everything)
  With roots:     Server can only read C:\Users\SriniAchuthan\OneDrive\Desktop\TypeScript\**

  ---
  How It Works

  1. Client declares roots to the server:

  {
    "roots": [
      {
        "uri": "file:///C:/Users/SriniAchuthan/OneDrive/Desktop/TypeScript",
        "name": "TypeScript Project"
      },
      {
        "uri": "file:///C:/Users/SriniAchuthan/Documents",
        "name": "Documents"
      }
    ]
  }

  2. Server receives roots and respects boundaries:

  import { Server } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js";
  import { ListRootsRequestSchema } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/types.js";

  const server = new Server({
    name: "my-server",
    version: "1.0.0",
  }, {
    capabilities: {
      roots: { listChanged: true }  // server supports roots
    }
  });

  // Server requests list of roots from client
  const roots = await server.request({
    method: "roots/list"
  });

  console.log(roots);
  // [{ uri: "file:///C:/Users/SriniAchuthan/...", name: "TypeScript Project" }]

  ---
  Root Notification (When Roots Change)

  When the user adds/removes a root, the client notifies the server:

  // Client sends this when roots change
  {
    "method": "notifications/roots/listChanged"
  }

  // Server listens and updates
  server.setNotificationHandler(
    RootsListChangedNotificationSchema,
    async () => {
      // Re-fetch updated roots
      const updatedRoots = await server.request({ method: "roots/list" });
      console.log("Roots updated:", updatedRoots);
    }
  );

  ---
  Types of Root URIs

  ┌───────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │     Type      │                Example                 │
  ├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Local file    │ file:///C:/Users/SriniAchuthan/Desktop │
  ├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ GitHub repo   │ git://github.com/user/repo             │
  ├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ HTTP resource │ https://api.example.com/data           │
  └───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Real World Example

  // File server that only reads within declared roots
  server.setRequestHandler(ReadFileRequestSchema, async (request) => {
    const filePath = request.params.path;

    // Check if path is within allowed roots
    const roots = await server.request({ method: "roots/list" });

    const isAllowed = roots.some(root =>
      filePath.startsWith(root.uri.replace("file://", ""))
    );

    if (!isAllowed) {
      throw new Error("Access denied — path outside allowed roots");
    }

    // Safe to read
    return { content: fs.readFileSync(filePath, "utf-8") };
  });

  ---
  Roots vs Tools vs Sampling

  ┌──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Feature  │                 Purpose                  │
  ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Tools    │ Server exposes functions Claude can call │
  ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Sampling │ Server asks Claude to generate text      │
  ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Roots    │ Client tells server where it can operate │
  └──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Summary

  Roots = Security boundaries for MCP servers

  Client defines roots  →  Server respects them  →  Safe file/resource access

  Roots ensure MCP servers only touch what they're supposed to — nothing more.

