● Find the Winning Player in Coin Game

  ---
  Problem Statement

  - There are N coins in a pile
  - Two players take turns (Player 1 goes first)
  - Each turn, a player can pick 1 or 2 coins
  - The player who picks the last coin wins
  - Both players play optimally
  - Who wins?

  ---
  Think Simply First

  ┌───────────┬──────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Coins (N) │  Winner  │                 Why                 │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 1         │ Player 1 │ Takes 1, wins immediately           │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 2         │ Player 1 │ Takes 2, wins immediately           │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 3         │ Player 2 │ Whatever P1 takes, P2 takes rest    │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 4         │ Player 1 │ Takes 1, leaves 3 for P2 (P2 loses) │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 5         │ Player 1 │ Takes 2, leaves 3 for P2 (P2 loses) │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 6         │ Player 2 │ Whatever P1 takes, P2 mirrors       │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 7         │ Player 1 │ Takes 1, leaves 6 for P2 (P2 loses) │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 8         │ Player 1 │ Takes 2, leaves 6 for P2 (P2 loses) │
  ├───────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 9         │ Player 2 │ Pattern repeats                     │
  └───────────┴──────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  The Pattern

  N = 1  → P1 wins
  N = 2  → P1 wins
  N = 3  → P2 wins  ← losing position
  N = 4  → P1 wins
  N = 5  → P1 wins
  N = 6  → P2 wins  ← losing position
  N = 7  → P1 wins
  N = 8  → P1 wins
  N = 9  → P2 wins  ← losing position

  Every multiple of 3 is a losing position for the current player.

  If N % 3 == 0  →  Player 2 wins
  If N % 3 != 0  →  Player 1 wins

  ---
  Why Does This Work?

  Key insight: If you leave your opponent with a multiple of 3, they always lose.

  Opponent picks 1  →  you pick 2  →  together = 3
  Opponent picks 2  →  you pick 1  →  together = 3

  So each round, 3 coins are removed — keeping the multiple of 3 intact for your opponent.

  ---
  Visual Walkthrough — N = 6

  Coins: ● ● ● ● ● ●   (6 coins)

  P1 picks 1 → ● ● ● ● ●  (5 left — P2's turn)
  P2 picks 2 → ● ● ●      (3 left — P1's turn)  ← P1 stuck at 3
  P1 picks 1 → ● ●        (2 left — P2's turn)
  P2 picks 2 → done!      P2 wins ✓

  P1 picks 2 → ● ● ● ●   (4 left — P2's turn)
  P2 picks 1 → ● ● ●     (3 left — P1's turn)  ← P1 stuck at 3 again
  P1 picks 1 → ● ●       (2 left — P2's turn)
  P2 picks 2 → done!      P2 wins ✓

  No matter what P1 does, P2 always wins when N=6.

  ---
  Visual Walkthrough — N = 7

  Coins: ● ● ● ● ● ● ●   (7 coins)

  P1 picks 1 → ● ● ● ● ● ●  (6 left — P2's turn)
               Now P2 is stuck at 6 (multiple of 3!)
               P1 wins ✓

  P1 simply reduces to a multiple of 3 for P2.

  ---
  Losing vs Winning Positions

  LOSING positions  (current player loses with optimal play):
  3, 6, 9, 12, 15 ...  → all multiples of 3

  WINNING positions (current player wins with optimal play):
  1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8 ... → everything else

  ---
  Optimal Strategy

  If N % 3 == 1  →  Pick 1 coin  (leaves multiple of 3 for opponent)
  If N % 3 == 2  →  Pick 2 coins (leaves multiple of 3 for opponent)
  If N % 3 == 0  →  You LOSE no matter what you pick

  ---
  Summary

  ┌────────────┬──────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Condition  │  Winner  │                Strategy                 │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ N % 3 == 0 │ Player 2 │ P2 mirrors P1 to maintain multiple of 3 │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ N % 3 == 1 │ Player 1 │ P1 picks 1, leaves multiple of 3        │
  ├────────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ N % 3 == 2 │ Player 1 │ P1 picks 2, leaves multiple of 3        │
  └────────────┴──────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

  The winner is whoever can always hand their opponent a multiple of 3.

